From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joinery <joinery@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 19:01:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Make the editor Joinery's: own theme, simpler flow, creation
 questionnaire

A Joinery colour preset, and this build's default: near-black neutral
surfaces so viewport content draws the eye, a brass accent matching the
Journeyman sidebar, contrast below the stock 0.3 to stop panel-on-panel
banding, softer corners and roomier spacing. The stock presets all stay
selectable.

The Project Manager is stripped back to opening and creating a project. Gone
are the disk scan, sorting, the per-project action sidebar, the view toggles,
the asset store, settings and donate. The logo becomes the name.

Creating a project walks steps: Godot's fields, the tools that will make it,
then the art bible in short themed pages. The first page keeps what you must
decide and loses what you must read, so the renderer's bullet list, its
information link and its "can be changed later" note are hidden: three
paragraphs about a choice with three options. "Edit Now" goes too, since it
asks whether to open a project you just asked to create.

Each tool names the MCP that drives it, starred for the strongest tier, which
is the thing actually being trusted rather than the program. Both
questionnaires come from the `joinery` CLI, so the catalog and the bible's
shape stay the one source of truth.

One size for the whole flow. Dropping reset_size stops the dialog resizing
between a wide first page and a narrow tool list, every input fills the same
width, and the stretch spacer is hidden on later pages where it only opened
gaps above and below the content.

Everything hides rather than deletes. Upstream still constructs and refers to
these controls, and removing their construction is how a fork earns a null
dereference that compiles cleanly and crashes on launch.

Joinery-only, not for upstream: it reroutes stock flows into Joinery's own.
